WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
Agent Hogan hadn’t seen what had grabbed&nbsp;
the commander, the sounds of blood-curdling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:11.700
screams had made his eyes instinctively snap&nbsp;
shut. Hogan kept both eyes tightly closed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.160
nothing but darkness blotting out his vision&nbsp;
even as the desperate, gravelly-voiced yells of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:23.940
the Commander filled his ears. Then, suddenly,&nbsp;
there was a scream, one that was abruptly cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:29.940
off. With a heavy thud, something dropped to the&nbsp;
ground, close enough to Agent Hogan’s feet that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.980
he could feel the vibrations of it hitting the&nbsp;
floor through his boots. It was the Commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.720
completely still… and silent.
Even though he’d been trained to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:46.200
look danger in the eye, Agent Hogan still didn’t&nbsp;
open his eyes, not even for a split second. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.420
was the first time that anything like this had&nbsp;
ever happened, and in the panic and confusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.600
his natural survival instincts had overridden the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation’s various rules and protocols in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:04.920
his head. And right now, something was telling&nbsp;
him not to look. That something was shifting and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.980
moving, just beyond Agent Hogan’s eyelids. He&nbsp;
could hear a shuffling as something much taller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:16.320
than an average human lumbered around.&nbsp;
It had been wailing before, shrieking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:24.540
uncontrollable and inconsolable despair, but now&nbsp;
its cries had lowered to a soft, sobbing whimper.&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.280
When someone’s eyes are closed, they aren’t&nbsp;
completely cut off from the world around them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.380
Just being unable to see things doesn’t&nbsp;
entirely dull a person’s awareness. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.380
people have a kind of sense, a spatial alert&nbsp;
that tells them what’s nearby even if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:46.980
can’t see it. And in this particular moment,&nbsp;
Agent Hogan’s was going haywire. The thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.980
the thing beyond his closed eyes, was moving&nbsp;
towards him, so close that he could feel its warm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:59.460
rancid breath on his skin. Just knowing it was so&nbsp;
close, hearing it breathe and sob gently, sensing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:05.100
it standing so close to him, it made the agent’s&nbsp;
already pounding heart rate start to climb.&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.300
He could feel beads of sweat crawling down&nbsp;
the side of his face as the creature leaned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.980
in closer. It almost felt like it was&nbsp;
trying to goad him into opening his eyes.&nbsp;

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:19.980
“Go on, just take a peek,” came a nagging,&nbsp;
gnawing voice at the back of Hogan’s mind.&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.560
He couldn’t help it; it was a natural human&nbsp;
response to be curious. But as intriguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:31.980
of a sight as it might have been, the creature&nbsp;
was far more terrifying; Hogan had no idea what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.200
horror he’d be faced with if he even opened&nbsp;
his eyes for a second. Little did he know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.320
forcing himself not to look – whether out of fear,&nbsp;
or pure survival instinct – had just saved the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:49.080
agent’s life. He could sense the creature turn and&nbsp;
start to slink off, getting further away again as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:55.260
it lumbered around a corner on its elongated legs.&nbsp;
Still, Agent Hogan waited a little longer before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.780
opening up his eyes again… just to be safe.
It had all started around a month earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.720
when the SCP Foundation had discovered a potential&nbsp;
anomaly was being housed by a group known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.540
Department of Abnormalities. You see, back then,&nbsp;
the SCP Foundation wasn’t quite the sprawling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.840
ginormous anomaly containment organization some&nbsp;
might be familiar with. It was far from being in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.360
its infancy at this point in time either, but the&nbsp;
Foundation was only just beginning to expand its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:30.000
collection of dangerous and unusual anomalies.
They had received word of an anomaly that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.740
being held in a facility in Stuttgart, Germany,&nbsp;
where Department of Abnormalities personnel had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.960
gone to great lengths to secure it. However,&nbsp;
despite all the measures they put in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.780
this creature was able to escape and didn’t leave&nbsp;
a single survivor in its wake after breaking free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:51.780
And so, being unfamiliar with this kind of&nbsp;
situation, the SCP Foundation had to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.220
what the best course of action would be.
The Foundation dispatched a retrieval team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.560
codenamed Zulu-9 A, to the facility that&nbsp;
the creature had broken free from. From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:07.260
the location in Stuttgart, Zulu-9 A was able to&nbsp;
track the anomaly's movements to an undisclosed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.940
mountainous region. It was believed that this&nbsp;
area was either the creature’s natural habitat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.560
where it had initially been captured, or&nbsp;
that the mountain at least closely resembled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.050
a place that the anomaly would recognize as&nbsp;
home. Flying in aboard a chopper, the Zulu-9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:27.120
A team assumed they could simply approach the&nbsp;
creature, and calmly coerce it long enough for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.720
them to capture and escort it back to base.
It turned out they were very, very wrong.&nbsp;

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.220
As their chopper landed nearby and the&nbsp;
retrieval team closed in on the creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.260
the captain of Zulu-9 A turned to talk to a&nbsp;
Foundation doctor that had accompanied the unit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:49.080
That one serendipitous action might have saved&nbsp;
his life… because it meant he wasn’t looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:55.260
the anomaly. A sudden, intense shriek rang out,&nbsp;
as the creature started crying, turning towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.700
the rest of the approached retrieval unit.
In a panic, they pulled their triggers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.280
weapons erupting into a hail of deafening&nbsp;
gunfire… but it had no effect. The creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.560
dashed towards them, shrugging off every&nbsp;
oncoming attack, completely unscathed. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.360
the Zulu-9 A team weren’t so well-defended…
By the time the captain turned back around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:21.720
the creature had already vanished, seemingly able&nbsp;
to move much faster than first anticipated. Maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.800
once they’d taken a look at it, the&nbsp;
retrieval team thought the creature’s long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.780
disproportioned limbs would make its movements&nbsp;
clumsy and cumbersome. Not that a single one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:36.060
of them was left standing to verify that.
The captain was left alone and stunned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.520
he hadn’t seen what had happened to his&nbsp;
team, only the aftermath… and it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:47.700
a pretty sight. Before long, backup arrived&nbsp;
in the form of Zulu-9’s B team. With the help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.560
of this secondary unit, the captain was able&nbsp;
to eventually capture the creature and remand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:59.700
it into SCP Foundation containment. How? By&nbsp;
placing a bag over its head. His theory was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.380
if it couldn’t see the retrieval team, it&nbsp;
couldn’t attack them. And while this strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.780
might have worked, he was only half-right.
Of course, the SCP Foundation’s mandate has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.940
been to contain and study the anomalies that it&nbsp;
encounters, and this was all the more prevalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:20.400
during these earlier days of the organization.&nbsp;
So, naturally, once the creature had seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:26.700
been captured and secured, a group of overzealous&nbsp;
and eager scientists were quick to step in so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:33.120
they could closely examine the anomaly. And for a&nbsp;
time, they were able to with little obstruction.&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.740
They could determine the basic details&nbsp;
about the creature’s physiology and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:43.920
that information logged in its very own file&nbsp;
on the SCP Foundation’s internal database. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:49.620
one scientist made a very fatal mistake during&nbsp;
those early tests and studies. Perhaps because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.300
the Foundation already had the creature captured&nbsp;
and secure within the walls of its own facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.680
they believed it posed no further threat&nbsp;
to personnel anymore. That it couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:05.940
hurt to remove the bag and take a closer look…
The very instant the bag was removed, chaos broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.280
loose. It was as if someone had lit the fuse of a&nbsp;
firework, only for it to explode before they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:16.380
rush to a safe enough distance away. Not only were&nbsp;
the scientists studying the creature horrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:21.720
by the sight of its grotesque, wide-jawed face,&nbsp;
but they were all seconds away from meeting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:28.380
same grim and grizzly fate as the retrieval team&nbsp;
sent to capture it. Now, they had seen its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:35.100
and the monster was loose within the Foundation.
At that particular point in time, the Foundation’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:39.720
early mistakes, this kind of thing was&nbsp;
unprecedented. It had never happened before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:46.500
not once – and nobody knew what to do. Most of the&nbsp;
anomalies they had encountered so far were benign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:52.140
Either harmless oddities, passive and non-hostile&nbsp;
creatures or artifacts with a few strange or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:58.320
unexplained properties but with no real sentience&nbsp;
to speak of. But this was a ferocious abomination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:05.880
intent on causing harm to the Foundation’s own&nbsp;
staff. So, someone needed to act, and do it fast.&nbsp;

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:11.220
A handful of armed Foundation agents happened to&nbsp;
be on site at the time, security officers Hendrix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:17.760
Hogan and Stevens as well as Sutton, the surviving&nbsp;
captain from the Zulu-9 A retrieval team. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.900
second that the creature was seen and began&nbsp;
retaliating against those that had seen its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.220
deafening alarms began to blare. That&nbsp;
was all the warning they would get;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.620
there were no Special Containment Procedures&nbsp;
yet, no protocol or standard procedure for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.300
something like this. The Foundation didn’t even&nbsp;
have a term for the situation that was going on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:41.280
although after today they would.
This was the SCP Foundation’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:45.060
first ever containment breach.
Given that he’d been involved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.500
retrieving the creature that was now on the loose,&nbsp;
Captain Sutton took the lead of the small group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:54.600
co-ordinating them as their de-facto commander.
“Don’t worry, we just need to get another bag over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.580
its head and get it back into containment,” he&nbsp;
instructed as calmly and confidently as he could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.180
given the tense circumstances.&nbsp;
“This thing is highly aggressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.820
but if you stay out of its sight, it won’t come&nbsp;
after you. Stay low, check your corners, and stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.260
frosty. We’ll split into twos and fan out, should&nbsp;
have more of a chance of catching him that way.”&nbsp;

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:16.980
Little did Sutton realize his misguided advice&nbsp;
would cost him, and others, their lives. Hendrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:20.820
and Stevens stumbled across the creature&nbsp;
first. Poking their heads around a corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:26.100
they spotted the tall, wiry humanoid shape&nbsp;
passing along the corridor that ran parallel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.720
to the one they were stood in. The two agents&nbsp;
saw the creature, but as far as they could tell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.060
it hadn’t seen them. But that didn’t matter.&nbsp;
Just as they both turned down the corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:40.680
ready to ambush the anomaly, the towering,&nbsp;
spindly form of the creature suddenly appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.360
behind them, screeching as it attacked.
Hearing the sounds of the others falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.160
prey to the monster, Captain Sutton and Agent&nbsp;
Hogan rushed towards the source of their fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.860
agents' screams. The de-facto Commander of the&nbsp;
group rushed a few paces ahead, confident that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:00.480
he knew how to get the upper hand against the&nbsp;
creature. Trailing behind him, Agent Hogan had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.060
been swept up in the panic of the whole situation.&nbsp;
He had no idea what this creature was, only that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:11.580
half of the retrieval team sent to capture it had&nbsp;
all died. Their weapons were useless against it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:17.040
which made the gun Hogan gripped in his trembling&nbsp;
fingers feel unreassuringly ineffective.&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.300
Sutton turned a corner down the corridor&nbsp;
ahead, and that had been when he locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:26.100
eyes with the creature. Seeing its face for&nbsp;
the first time; he hadn’t even caught a proper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.960
glimpse of it when he’d managed to capture&nbsp;
it in the mountains. But now he had seen it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.840
and it knew. From around the corner, unable to&nbsp;
see Sutton or the monster, Agent Hogan let the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:43.320
panic get the better of him, and closed his eyes.&nbsp;
An action that had unknowingly saved his life.&nbsp;

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.600
Once the monster had passed him by, and Agent&nbsp;
Hogan had dared to open up his eyes again, his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:54.300
mind started racing. It had seen him, that much&nbsp;
he was certain of; even with his eyes closed, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.300 --> 00:11:00.240
had sensed just how close to him the creature had&nbsp;
gotten to him. It had to have seen him, and yet he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:06.420
was still alive. Sutton had said it would attack&nbsp;
anyone that it saw… unless Sutton had got it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:12.900
wrong. The creature was attacking anyone it looked&nbsp;
at; it was going after anyone who looked at it.&nbsp;

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.280
It was learning that key difference that&nbsp;
allowed Agent Hogan to gain the upper hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:21.540
on the creature. However, he quickly realized&nbsp;
that the number of people in the facility was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:26.820
rapidly dwindling. The longer the anomaly was able&nbsp;
to roam free, the more Foundation personnel would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:32.940
see its face, and fall victim to its rage. Agent&nbsp;
Hogan realized what he would have to do, sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:38.220
out a message to the SCP Foundation higher-ups&nbsp;
that this anomaly was only attacking anyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.020
looked at it. Then, he sealed himself in the&nbsp;
chamber where the scientists had been examining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:47.700
the creature. They’d filmed the examination;&nbsp;
all Hogan had to do was watch the part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:53.100
footage where the bag was removed, and look&nbsp;
at the anomaly’s face. The monster took care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.100 --> 00:12:00.240
of the rest. Traveling at impossible speeds,&nbsp;
SCP-096 appeared in the room behind Agent Hogan.&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.520
Ever since, the SCP Foundation has poured&nbsp;
innumerable resources into ensuring they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.740
have enough personnel who are specially trained&nbsp;
to deal with containment breaches. Some will even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:16.320
tell you that it was this first incident that&nbsp;
led to the idea of Mobile Task Forces, teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:21.000
that the Foundation can deploy within moments&nbsp;
to respond to anomalies breaking out of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.220
containment. Of course, who really knows if that’s&nbsp;
really true? Maybe half of it actually happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:31.980
and the rest was built off rumors and hearsay.
If you sign up to join one of the SCP Foundation’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:37.020
Mobile Task Forces nowadays, then perhaps your&nbsp;
commanding officer will tell you this story during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:42.480
your basic training. It could be they’re trying to&nbsp;
intimidate you, or just reemphasize how important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.300
the role of the MTFs is within the Foundation.&nbsp;
One thing is for sure, though; anyone assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:56.100
to guard SCP-096’s chamber knows to close their&nbsp;
eyes in the event of another containment breach.&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:04.020
Now go and check out “SCP-096 THE SHY GUY (SCP&nbsp;
ANIMATION)” and “SCP-096'S SAD END!” for more!

